[{"id":"ns-order-cycle","itemId":"next-state-logic","role":"warmup","promptHtml":"In lesson 5.2's two-block door, put these in the order they happen when a closed door is pushed.","explanationHtml":"<p><code class=\"nobreak\">next</code> is logic: it changes as soon as <code class=\"nobreak\">push</code> does, before any edge (lesson 5.2). The state register waits for the rising edge, then takes <code class=\"nobreak\">next</code>, and <code class=\"nobreak\">is_open</code>, which depends only on the state, follows at once. Putting the edge before <code class=\"nobreak\">next</code> treats <code class=\"nobreak\">next</code> as a register; it is the value the register is about to take.</p>","type":"order","steps":["`push` becomes 1 while the door is closed.","`next` becomes `OPEN`.","A rising edge of `clk` comes.","The state register takes its new value.","`is_open` becomes 1."],"shuffled":[2,0,3,4,1]},{"id":"ns-order-default","itemId":"next-state-logic","role":"core","promptHtml":"Put these steps of lesson 5.2's two-block door in order, from the first line of the next-state logic to the edge.","explanationHtml":"<p>The default line comes first, so the <code class=\"nobreak\">case</code> can change <code class=\"nobreak\">next</code> where a transition applies, and <code class=\"nobreak\">next</code> keeps the state everywhere else (lessons 1.4 and 5.2). Only at the edge does the register take <code class=\"nobreak\">next</code>. Placed after the <code class=\"nobreak\">case</code>, the default line would undo every transition: <code class=\"nobreak\">next</code> would always equal <code class=\"nobreak\">state</code>, and the door would never move.</p>","type":"order","steps":["`next` is given the value of `state`.","The `case` picks the line for the present state.","That line's `if` may give `next` another value.","At the rising edge, `state` takes `next`."],"shuffled":[2,3,1,0]},{"id":"ns-find-bug","itemId":"next-state-logic","role":"stretch","promptHtml":"This two-block door should behave like lesson 5.1's: it stays in its state until <code class=\"nobreak\">push</code> or <code class=\"nobreak\">key</code> moves it. Its testbench fails. Pick the line that is wrong.","explanationHtml":"<p>The first line of the next-state logic gives <code class=\"nobreak\">next</code> the value <code class=\"nobreak\">CLOSED</code> instead of <code class=\"nobreak\">state</code>. So wherever no transition applies, the door goes to <code class=\"nobreak\">CLOSED</code> at the next edge: an open door shuts itself and a locked one unlocks. Three other lines also end in <code>next = CLOSED;</code>, the two transitions and <code class=\"nobreak\">default</code>, and those are right. Written <code>next = state;</code>, the default keeps the door where it is.</p>","type":"find-bug","file":"hdl/level-05/door2_bug/door2_bug.sv","firstLine":1,"lines":[[{"text":"// The door of lesson 5.1, in the two-block form. push should open a closed","color":"var(--code-token-comment)","bold":false}],[{"text":"// door and close an open one; key should lock a closed door and unlock a","color":"var(--code-token-comment)","bold":false}],[{"text":"// locked one. push and key are one-cycle pulses; push wins if both come","color":"var(--code-token-comment)","bold":false}],[{"text":"// at once.","color":"var(--code-token-comment)","bold":false}],[{"text":"module","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":" ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"door2_bug","color":"var(--code-token-function)","bold":false},{"text":" (","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}],[{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"input","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":true},{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"logic","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":" clk,","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}],[{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{